   "source": [
    "# Test Result Analysis of a 4-bit Adder\n",
    "\n",
    "**Course:** Python Programming (Individual Case Study)\n",
    "**Name:** Ishita Khare\n"
    "**Department:** EEE"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e13d4c19",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": []
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "68828410",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Problem Statement\n",
    "\n",
    "In digital design, a circuit is checked by applying test cases and comparing the **expected output** with the **actual output** of the circuit. This is the basic idea of *verification*.\n",
    "\n",
    "This case study analyses the pass/fail results of test cases applied to a **4-bit adder** and finds which kinds of inputs make the adder fail.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2. Objectives\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Store test cases (inputs, expected output, actual output) in lists.\n",
    "2. Compare the expected and actual output for every test case.\n",
    "3. Count the passes and failures and calculate the pass percentage.\n",
    "4. Show the results using charts and explain the cause of the failures.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 3. Dataset Description\n",
    "\n",
    "- The data is **simulated** (created inside this notebook).\n",
    "- A 4-bit adder takes two inputs, **A** and **B**, each from 0 to 15, and gives a 4-bit sum. The largest value a 4-bit output can hold is 15.\n",
    "- 20 test cases are used: normal values, zero values, and corner cases such as 15 + 0, 8 + 7 and 15 + 15.\n",
    "- **Expected sum** = A + B .\n",
    "- **Actual sum** = output of a faulty adder whose carry is lost, so it only keeps the lower 4 bits of the result.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4. Methodology\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Create the list of test inputs (A, B).\n",
    "2. Find the expected sum for each test.\n",
    "3. Find the actual sum using the faulty adder function.\n",
    "4. Compare expected and actual and mark each test PASS or FAIL.\n",
    "5. Count the results and find the pass percentage.\n",
    "6. Plot the results and write observations."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "64e89833",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Python Implementation\n",
    "### 5.1 Test data"
