   "source": [
    "## 7. Observations and Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "**Observations**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Out of **20** test cases, **12 passed** and **8 failed**, giving a pass rate of **60 %**.\n",
    "2. All 8 failures occur when **A + B is greater than 15**. These are the overflow (carry) cases, such as 8 + 8, 9 + 9 and 15 + 15.\n",
    "3. Every test where A + B is 15 or less passed. The largest passing sum is **15** and the smallest failing sum is **16**, so the bug starts exactly where the sum no longer fits in 4 bits.\n",
    "4. In the failing cases the actual output is the correct sum minus 16 (for example 16 gives 0 and 30 gives 14). This shows that the **carry-out bit is being lost**.\n",
    "5. Corner cases such as 15 + 0, 8 + 7 and 12 + 3 (sum exactly 15) pass, while 8 + 8 and 15 + 1 (sum 16) fail. Testing only small, simple inputs would have missed this bug, so **corner cases are important in verification**.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion**\n",
    "\n",
    "The 4-bit adder works correctly only when the result fits within 4 bits. The adder is not fully correct because it does not handle the carry. A fix would be to add a 5th output bit (carry-out) , after which the same test cases should be run again to confirm that all tests pass. This case study shows, in a simple way, how comparing expected and actual outputs helps find the pattern behind a bug, which is the basic idea of design verification.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Acknowledgement:** All data is simulated and created by me. No external dataset was used."
   ]
